    ! I spent an hour trying to break a tool I had built for a youth volleyball club.
    ! Not one of those breaks was in the layer I had spent the entire design protecting.
    ! Then I built exactly that — writes land at exact byte positions behind a content hash, and nothing a model says reaches a page without code agreeing.
    ! And I was still staring at a screen that lied to me.
    ! It reported failure for work that was live in front of me.
    ! I had specified safety.
    ! I had not specified truthfulness.
    ! The mental model I brought to breaking my own tool was the wrong one.
    ! With the inversion unnamed, my operational knowledge had nothing to attach to but resemblance.
    ! The spec does bound model calls — six turns, on the component that answers questions, the one that looked like the diagrams I had studied.
    ! The layers that came from something I measured going wrong survived an hour of deliberate abuse.
    · I broke it seven times.
    · Last October I published a post arguing that agent safety is an architecture problem: the model proposes, hard-coded code disposes, a human approves.
    · I hit refresh mid-apply — the oldest bug in web development — and the write path caught the resubmit precisely as designed.
    · What let me miss it is an inversion my spec never named.
    · Mine runs the opposite direction.
    · Everything good about mine comes from that flip — a plan can be stored, resumed, audited, unit-tested.
    · Here is the test I would now run on anything an agent helped build.

